WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:06.300
This is a story about
one of the most audacious

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.373
financial frauds in history.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.970
A very complicated conspiracy

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.690
involving a $100 million oil tanker

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.083
and also the murder of someone involved.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.660
We now know that David
Mockett had sort of stumbled

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.013
into one of the most lucrative
maritime frauds in history.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.830
He was assassinated with a bomb

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.303
that was meant only for him.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.450
This is the story of a single oil tanker

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.340
that was attacked and burned

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.890
in what everyone thought
was a piracy incident,

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.433
but turned out to be something
much darker and stranger.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.710
My assumption was that the days

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.170
of Pirates of the Caribbean
are ancient history

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.540
and everything cleans up.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.540
But the more we dug into what
happened to this one ship,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.240
the more criminality we found.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.223
This story really exposed
just how corrupt and lawless,

00:01:08.223 --> 00:01:11.823
frankly, the global
shipping industry can be.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.460
My name's Kit Chellel.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.620
I'm a senior writer with Bloomberg

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.320
and with Matthew Campbell,
we are the authors

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.607
of "Dead In The Water."

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.160
Brillante Virtuoso was kind of a workhorse

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.090
of international trade.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.580
It was like thousands of other oil tankers

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.830
just taking a million
barrels of from one port

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.980
to another over and over
again, several times a year.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.220
And the Brillante was very large.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.400
It was a 275-meter-long vessel,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.590
which believe it or not,

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:56.190
does not put it among the
very, very largest oil tankers

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.960
and container ships, but it's huge.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.660
It's almost three football fields.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.790
And on this particular journey,

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.610
it was heading from Ukraine to China

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.490
and had just passed through the Suez Canal

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.853
and was sailing out into the Gulf of Aden.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.060
Now, the Gulf of Aden sits
between Yemen in the north

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.090
and the Horn of Africa,
Somalia, in the south.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.720
And at the time of this
voyage, summer of 2011,

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.940
piracy was at its absolute height.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:28.650
There have been two more pirate attacks

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.060
off the coast of Africa,

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.510
bringing the number to
eight in the past 12 days.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.970
What used to go down several
times a week is that,

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.850
you know, little fast-moving
boat carrying seven

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.000
or eight guys with AK-47s
would come up alongside

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.470
these gigantic cargo ships

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.590
carrying hundreds of millions
of dollars worth of cargo.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.780
And they would, you know,
clamber their way aboard,

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.840
take control of the vessel and
then take it back to Somalia

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.913
and demand millions of dollars in ransom.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.590
Any sailors on board such a vessel

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.130
would've known that they were
heading into waters where

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:05.070
if pirates succeeded in getting aboard,

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.350
they and their vessel
could be taken hostage

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.870
and perhaps taken to
Somalia and held there

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:13.260
for ransom for months or even years.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.660
Which is why there was this extra measure

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:16.710
taken for security.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.990
They decided they needed
a secure, you know,

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.940
a security detail to go with
them through the Gulf of Aden.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.500
It was nearly midnight,

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.400
and there was a couple
of sailors on watch,

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.760
both Filipino guys, and
they spotted on the radar

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.323
a small craft approaching.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.310
At first they had no idea what
it was or what was going on,

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.110
and of course they were terrified.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.050
But when the small boat approached

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.200
they used a loud speaker to shout down,

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.027
and the guys on the little vessel said,

00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:54.330
"We're security. We're
the security detail.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.907
Can you lower your ladder
so we can come aboard?"

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.620
Within seconds of them
arriving on the ship,

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.630
they started waving their rifles around

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:05.280
and commandeered the vessel,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.980
and it became clear very quickly

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.130
this was a terrible
situation for all the crew

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.563
who were rounded up and
locked in a TV room.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.050
And then for the next couple of hours,

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.790
they were sort of stuck in darkness,

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.310
just listening and waiting, you know,

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.320
dreading what might be happening.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.020
Their worst fear was that
they'd be taken off to Somalia

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.400
and locked in some prison,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.290
awaiting ransom payment to be paid.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.443
So they were all, you
know, completely terrified.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.560
The ship started to move

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.600
and then there was a loud explosion,

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.000
and smoke started pouring into the TV room

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.830
where all the crew were waiting.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:43.860
And when they came out, they
saw that these armed men

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.980
had disappeared, apparently fled,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.960
and left the Brillante Virtuoso

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.573
carrying a million barrels of oil burning.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:02.250
So when a large commercial vessel

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.640
is destroyed or seriously damaged,

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.480
this whole industry of
people swings into action.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.290
And that's because a
damaged commercial vessel

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:15.543
that might be carrying,
you know, 100, 200,

00:05:15.543 --> 00:05:18.120
$300 million of cargo or more,

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.840
that might itself be worth
$100 million or more.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.390
It is also, above all, a liability.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.630
That vessel is insured,
and that insurance policy

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:31.233
could be valued in the many,
many, many millions of dollars.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.050
So among the very first
people who were brought in

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.000
when there is a big marine accident,

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.700
are what's called maritime surveyors.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:45.690
And a maritime surveyor
is kind of like the person

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.580
the insurance company would send

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.400
if you had a tree fall on your house.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.680
The insurance company
needs to, first of all,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.500
make sure that a tree actually
did fall on your house,

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.920
assess the damage, figure
out if you, you know,

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.710
perhaps pushed the tree onto
your house intentionally,

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:05.550
write up a report, and
basically, all being well,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.230
that report is favorable

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.210
and then your claim is paid.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:13.020
So a marine surveyor essentially
does that on massive scale.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.150
And in the case of the Brillante Virtuoso,

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.940
there was a very good marine surveyor,

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:21.240
a British guy who happened
to live in Yemen, in Aden,

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:24.190
which was the nearest city to
where this incident occurred.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:30.990
So David Mockett is this
big, brash confident guy

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:35.160
with a booming laugh who
lives an expat life in Yemen.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.510
And he knows the area very well,

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.760
he's been there for a long time.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:40.830
And he's also, you know,
an expert in shipping.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:45.030
He was once a master mariner
with a commercial navy,

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:47.220
and he's got decades of
experience doing this work.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.680
So, you know, it's a problem,

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.660
it's difficult to try
and get out to a vessel

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:53.970
10 miles off the coast
of Yemen that's ablaze.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.290
But for someone like David Mockett,

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.630
it's something he's more
than capable of doing.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.610
So he hitches a ride on a fishing trawler

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.773
to get out to the Brillante Virtuoso.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.680
But he did have some initial trouble

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.200
getting on board the Brillante.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.700
It was a bit slow to get permission.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.490
And the way that works
is that when there is

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.970
a marine accident, typically
a vessel then becomes

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:17.763
the property of what's
called a salvage crew.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:24.210
And salvers are the 911
service of the high seas.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.410
When a vessel runs into
trouble, comes to grief,

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.050
salvage crews race to the scene to try

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.500
and keep it from sinking,
pull it off rocks,

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.210
whatever needs to be done.

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:39.720
And from that point on, the
ship is the responsibility

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.173
of salvage crews, who ultimately
work for the ship's owners.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.910
I think David Mockett
would've known pretty early on

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:56.220
that there was something suspicious

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.653
about what happened to
the Brillante Virtuoso.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.650
One of the things he noticed was

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.070
there was no evidence of a
rocket-propelled grenade strike.

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:10.890
Supposedly an RPG had started the fire.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.140
David saw no evidence of this.

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:17.610
He was confused by the
fact that the pirate attack

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.620
had occurred in the middle of the night,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.380
which is not typically how
Somali pirates operate.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.500
They would usually strike in daylight.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.650
He didn't notice any evidence
of weapons being discharged,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.570
bullet holes or anything like that,

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.970
which again, was was part of the story.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:36.810
So he didn't have any positive
theory of what had occurred.

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:40.650
He just had certain suspicions that

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:44.523
there was more to it than the
initial accounts suggested.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.770
And when he arrived and
spoke to the salvage crew,

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:52.410
their attitude was kind of strange.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.020
They sat down and had dinner one evening,

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:58.260
and the salvors were
quizzing David Mockett

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:01.140
about what he was doing
in a place like Yemen.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.607
And the Greek salvage captain said to him,

00:09:03.607 --> 00:09:05.100
"You should be at home
with your grandchildren.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.050
You shouldn't be in a place like this."

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.260
David Mockett went back
to his office in Aden

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.480
and started the process
of sending his reports

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.310
back to London so the insurers

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.380
could get a clear idea of what happened.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.450
And in those early reports, you can see

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:26.790
that he's suspicious
about the whole thing.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:30.097
And indeed he tells his employers,

00:09:30.097 --> 00:09:31.680
"I need to go and find
out what's happened."

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.260
He's going to go and talk to
his local government contacts.

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.120
He's going to dig a bit deeper

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.010
and try and find out the true story,

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:39.910
but he never gets a chance to do that.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:47.873
So on July 20, 2011, about
two weeks after the attack

00:09:47.873 --> 00:09:51.900
and fire that all but destroyed
the Brillante Virtuoso,

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:56.310
David was working in his
office in Aden as usual.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:59.700
He typically got to work
very early in the morning,

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:01.410
you know, between 7 and 8.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.440
He would work until lunchtime,
and he would go home,

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.690
have lunch at home, stay
home for a few hours

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:09.870
and then return to work later in the day.

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:14.430
And, totally normal morning,
he drove to his office, parked.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:17.419
And when it came time
to go home for lunch,

00:10:17.419 --> 00:10:22.220
he got up, walked out the hall,
climbed into his Lexus SUV

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:26.700
and turned the key,
drove out of the car park

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:30.870
of this small office building,
where he had his workspace,

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:33.150
and then onto a very busy road,

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:34.770
one of the main roads of Aden, actually.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:37.770
And he hadn't gone more
than a couple hundred meters

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.630
when a bomb that had been placed

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:41.400
under the driver's seat detonated,

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:42.500
killing him instantly.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.480
There wasn't a big explosion.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:53.100
It was a very targeted
incident in a way that really,

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:56.070
you know, people afterwards
could only explain

00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:57.210
as being assassination.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:00.183
Whoever placed that bomb
wanted to kill David Mockett.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:07.800
So the British government
did take some action,

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:10.980
as it does when a Briton is killed abroad.

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:14.430
The Foreign Office did try and
ascertain the circumstances

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:15.810
of what had occurred.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:20.160
Ultimately, the Met police
sent an investigator to Aden

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.140
to liaise with the Yemeni
police, but really,

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:28.230
there was not much the
British government could do.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:31.440
Yemen at this time was
incredibly dangerous.

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:34.200
There were no British
diplomats in Aden whatsoever.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.050
There was no British presence at all.

00:11:38.287 --> 00:11:39.420
You know, after the
death of David Mockett,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.000
there was a there was an
inquest into his death,

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:45.750
and the coroner found that
he'd been unlawfully killed

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.100
but didn't know who had done it.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.320
There was no evidence as
to who had killed him,

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:50.970
but there was evidence at that inquest

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:53.730
that he'd been targeted because he was

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:57.120
on the verge of uncovering
a criminal conspiracy.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:58.650
But after that finding, you know,

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:01.590
there was no police action.
There was no full investigation.

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:03.390
It was all just sort of forgotten about.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.843
The only thing that was left
was an insurance dispute.

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:18.480
So when a large vessel
like the Brillante Virtuoso

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.190
is destroyed, or effectively
destroyed in this case,

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:26.670
insurance companies,
specifically insurers who operate

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:29.250
through the Lloyd's of
London insurance market,

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.560
are on the hook for a great deal of money,

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.600
perhaps hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:36.720
In this case, the vessel itself,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:38.580
what's called the hull insurance,

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:42.210
was worth about $80 million.

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:45.330
There was also $100
million in cargo insurance

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:48.360
because the oil on board
was insured separately

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:49.620
from the vessel itself.

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.230
So this is a very large liability,

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:54.990
even for big insurance
companies, even at Lloyd's,

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:57.570
which is the world's most
important insurance market

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:00.333
where they deal in huge transactions.

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:05.490
And of course, if you are on
the hook for that much money

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.100
you generally would
not want to pay it out,

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:12.330
unless you're very confident
that the claim is legitimate.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:16.320
In practice, they might settle,
you know maybe pay you 50%,

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:20.070
or maybe come to some
other amicable arrangement.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:23.490
What they're very unlikely
to do is to go to court

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.260
to try and fight you about it.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:28.260
However, in the case
of Brillante Virtuoso,

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.510
these London insurers,

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:33.180
looking at this huge bill
with which they were faced

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:36.000
and looking at the circumstances
of what had occurred,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.270
and also taking into account
the death of David Mockett,

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:40.650
who was one of their own,

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:44.280
a creature of the Lloyd's world
and of the maritime world,

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:48.843
they decided to fight and
to go nose-to-nose in court.

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:54.120
And this insurance dispute had become,

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:55.713
you know, overtly hostile.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:14:00.750
The Greek ship owner is a
guy called Marios Iliopoulos,

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.210
and they decided they weren't
going to pay his claim

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.190
on the basis that they didn't believe

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:07.200
it was a genuine act of piracy.

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:09.330
And so it sets the scene for

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:12.480
this high-stakes London lawsuit,

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:14.790
which turns out to be the
best chance we're going to get

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:16.790
to really understand what happened here.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:28.290
The owner specifically was a
man named Marios Iliopoulos,

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:31.863
who was a prominent Greek shipping tycoon,

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:34.560
owned a fleet of oil tankers,

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.960
also the owner of
something called Seajets,

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:40.230
which is one of the most
popular fast-ferry services

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:43.383
linking Athens to places
like Mykonos and Santorini.

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:52.230
And one of the fun things to
know about Marios Iliopoulos

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:53.613
is he has a hobby.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:59.100
He's a rally driver and a
very successful rally driver.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.370
He's known in the rally
world as Super Mario.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.923
He tends to do quite well in rally races.

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:12.930
Really a pursuit that
requires nerves of steel

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:15.330
and a certain disregard for risk,

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:18.603
which would probably come in
handy in the shipping business.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:25.560
Even years after the event, no one had got

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:28.560
a satisfactory story out of the captain

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.780
or the chief engineer of the ship.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.430
No one had sat down and
interviewed the salvage crew.

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.030
Any evidence of what happened to the ship

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:36.870
had been destroyed.

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:40.590
The ship was sent off to a
scrapyard and torn to pieces.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:43.050
So the insurers had an enormous
challenge on their hands

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:45.420
to prove that this was an act of fraud

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:46.923
rather than an act of piracy.

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:51.330
When you're seeking to invalidate in court

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:53.340
an $80 million insurance claim,

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:56.070
or a nearly $80 million insurance claim,

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.050
you need a huge amount of evidence.

00:15:58.050 --> 00:15:59.400
And one of the things you do

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:01.140
is you hire private investigators.

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:05.520
And there is a small
universe of these guys,

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:09.990
typically ex-police, who do
a lot of insurance cases.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:13.620
So in this case, there were
two private investigators

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:16.413
who were hired named Richard
Veale and Michael Conner.

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:21.300
And they were digging
deeper and deeper into

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:22.320
what had happened to the ship

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.480
and what happened to David Mockett.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.130
And they were doing, you know,

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:27.390
the work that should have been done

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:29.010
in the immediate aftermath of the attack,

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:32.340
which is find the sailors,
find the maritime officials,

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:34.080
find the witnesses to what happened,

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:36.030
and talk to them and get their stories.

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:42.916
One of the whistle-blowers
who came forward to tell the

00:16:42.916 --> 00:16:46.080
truth about the fraud on
the Brillante Virtuoso

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:48.240
was working with the salvage crew

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:51.450
and arrived at the scene
shortly after the attack,

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:54.960
and was there as the salvage crew ensured

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.700
that the fire didn't go out,

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:58.620
that the ship was completely destroyed

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:01.950
and that the insurance fraud
had a better chance of success.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:03.390
But because he was based in Yemen,

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:04.830
because he knew the ship owner

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:08.460
and because he knew the
the salvage captain,

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:10.110
had known them for several months,

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:11.910
he witnessed all these events happening.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:13.140
He witnessed the planning.

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:16.740
He knew that a vessel was
going to come to Yemeni waters

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:20.100
and be attacked and destroyed
in an insurance fraud.

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:22.740
He had absolutely vital
information for the police

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:25.080
and for the insurance investigators.

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:27.333
But he put his life at
risk by revealing it.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:33.630
He had a life in Greece, and
he had to flee in terror.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:36.450
You know, he had to get
taken away by armed guards

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:37.950
and put on a plane to the UK.

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:40.920
And, you know, when he
gave evidence in court,

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:43.860
the Greek whistle-blower was
flanked by police officers

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:45.000
from an unknown location.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.750
Like, he's still living in hiding.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.660
They also were able to
interview a member of the crew

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:55.320
who admitted later on that
he had faked his story

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:58.110
because he'd been threatened
by Marios Iliopoulos.

00:17:58.110 --> 00:18:01.320
So piecing all this evidence together,

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:04.110
they were able to assemble
a body of evidence

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:07.653
that they felt was strong enough
to really fight this case.

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:12.390
The judge takes a few weeks
to hand down his decision,

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:14.400
but when he does, you know,
it's a damning indictment

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:15.570
of the ship owner.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:18.780
He says that Marios Iliopoulos
is a dishonest witness,

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:22.350
that he disgracefully threatened
members of the courts,

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:24.900
and that he's not telling
the truth about, you know,

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:29.640
what happens to the ship and
what happens in the lawsuits.

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:31.350
And his misconduct is so serious

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.843
that this claim is thrown out.

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:42.214
A prosecution of David
Mockett's murder by the UK

00:18:42.214 --> 00:18:45.990
was always gonna be a very long shot.

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:50.670
This crime, the murder,
occurred in a faraway land

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:54.570
where Britain has no presence,
obviously no jurisdiction.

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:59.070
So expecting a British
prosecution for that act,

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.500
I think was always
going to be a long shot.

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:04.080
What I am much more surprised by

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:07.020
is that no British law enforcement agency

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:09.600
has seriously pursued a prosecution

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:14.600
in what a London judge
has now ruled was a fraud.

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:19.350
One of the reasons that
the police struggled

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:22.950
to get to grips with what
happens in maritime fraud

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:26.430
is that it takes place in the cracks

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:29.523
between the traditional
areas of law enforcement.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:35.610
In this case, you had a
Greek-owned ship carrying oil

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:38.580
that was being traded
out of Cyprus to China,

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:41.430
crewed by Filipino sailors,

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.250
flying the flag of Liberia in West Africa.

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:46.440
So you've got seven or eight
different nation states

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.480
already involved, and the question is,

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:49.860
who prosecutes this crime?

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:52.380
Whose job it is to deal with this?

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:54.690
And the reality of law enforcement

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:56.430
is that actually no one wants the job.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:20:00.210
It's incredibly difficult to investigate

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:03.513
and prosecute any fraud
that happens out at sea.

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:08.790
And so most police forces around the world

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:10.350
just decide that it's not for them to do,

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:11.910
and they can't do it.

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.400
And in the case of the Brillante Virtuoso,

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:17.820
the police were interested.
They were given evidence

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:19.620
that something untoward had happened,

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:22.200
and they decided it was
going to be too difficult

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:23.583
to prosecute a case.

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.140
No one has ever been prosecuted.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:30.450
No one has ever been charged

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:34.110
for a very significant fraud

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:36.090
that was attempted against
the Lloyd's market,

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:38.913
one of Britain's most important
financial institutions.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:45.090
David Mockett spent a
lifetime either working at sea

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:47.850
or in far-flung ports around the world.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.130
And, you know, he was
looking forward to spending,

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.710
finally spending some time
with his wife and his daughters

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:55.050
and their grandchildren who loved him.

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:57.780
And, you know, the tragedy
of the Brillante Virtuoso

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:00.580
is that he was killed before
he had a chance to do that.

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:09.240
Iliopoulos did actually do OK financially,

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:11.340
out of this, believe it or not.

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:13.440
One of the things that
happened was that the bank,

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:15.960
which had loaned the money to Iliopoulos

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:20.250
to buy the Brillante
Virtuoso, wrote off the debt

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:21.600
in the course of this litigation,

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:23.310
concluding that it would
never be recovered.

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:27.240
So from the debt side,
he was free and clear.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:30.780
By destroying the vessel
with a fire, he was able to,

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:34.737
you know, escape debts of in
the region of $60, $70 million,

00:21:34.737 --> 00:21:38.370
and the ship was losing
enormous amount of money

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:39.870
and was coming to towards
the end of its life.

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:43.680
So from a purely financial point of view,

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:45.240
destroying the Brillante Virtuoso

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:46.943
was the best thing he could have done.

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:50.790
Part of me has expected
people in the shipping world

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:53.520
to come out and say, "It's not that bad.

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:55.290
You know, it's not as bad as this one ship

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:58.020
makes it appear to be."
But no one's done that.

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:00.960
And, you know, unfortunately I think,

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:05.010
there is this criminal
underbelly to global shipping

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:06.420
that is thriving.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:07.980
And there seems to be very little

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:09.600
that anyone can do about it.

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:12.050
And the more you dig, the
worse it seems to look.

